  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1052 AM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nToday Through Sunday... \nToday will have fair weather throughout the \nregion, above normal temperatures will range from the low to mid 70s \nwith mostly sunny skies. Things begin to change as we head into \nSaturday. A upper level trough will push through the Rockies. This \nwill bring breezy to low end windy conditions our area tomorrow. \nGusts will be between 25-35 mph in lowland areas with gusts near \nadvisory threshold, up to 45-50 mph, in higher elevation areas. \nTomorrow as the trough passes upper level winds will veer to the \nNorthwest briefly pushing cooler air back into the area into next \nweek. Highs will range in the low to mid 60s with some broken low \nlevel clouds developing by tomorrow afternoon. On Sunday we will see \nthe coldest temps of the season with lows dropping into the low to \nmid 30s bringing possible first freezes to some lowland areas. The \none thing that may keep temps from dropping below freezing is \nkeeping just enough wind through the night preventing a quick drop \ntoward sunrise. Upper level ridging will build back into the area \ncreating surface high pressure going into next week. This will bring \nmostly clear skies and a drop off in breezy winds. \n\nMonday Through Thursday... \nHigh pressure will begin to exit the \nregion, briefly, Monday afternoon and evening as a quick moving \nupper level trough swings by. This will bring a low-end chance for a \nstray shower or two during this timeframe but kept PoPs below \nmentionable. High pressure returns for most of the remaining \ntimeframe. Highs most days will be near normal in the low to mid 60s \nwith fair skies. Thursday a upper level low/trough will work into \nthe area, right now there is model disagreement on the track and \nintensity. The GFS has remained consistent in developing a deep \nclosed low over the southern Rockies while the EC is much more \nprogressive.  The 06Z GEFS ensemble mean was very close to the \ndeterministic and ensemble mean of the EC.  Right now thinking is \nthe EC is more on track which will keep any precipitation and cold \nair off to our east.",
